::<MH>() [line 71-75]\n      └─> MerkleTreeConstructor::new_merkle_tree() [dispatch trait]\n        └─> MerkleTreeGpu::new_generic_with_hash::<MH>() \n          [/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs:113]\n          └─> MH::compress_rows() [line 130]\n            └─> poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes() \n              [/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/merkle_tree.rs:52]\n              └─> _poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes() [FFI call to CUDA launcher]\n```\n\nThe Rust bindings are in:\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/merkle_tree.rs` lines 52-68\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/hash_scheme.rs` lines 118-148 (dispatch layer)\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs` lines 113-172 (tree constructor)\n\n### 3. What determines 584 instances?\n\nThe 584 kernel instances are determined by **the number of Merkle tree layers** times **kernel calls in the tree-building loop**:\n\n**From merkle_tree.rs lines 127-160:**\n```rust\nlet mut query_digest_layer = DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(query_stride);\nunsafe {\n    MH::compress_rows(...) // CALL 1: Hash rows into first layer\n}\n\nlet mut digest_layers = vec![query_digest_layer];\nwhile digest_layers.last().unwrap().len() > 1 {\n    let prev_layer = digest_layers.last().unwrap();\n    let mut layer = DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(prev_layer.len() / 2);\n    unsafe {\n        MH::compress_layer(&mut layer, prev_layer, layer_len) // CALL 2+: Build tree layers\n    }\n    digest_layers.push(layer);\n}\n```\n\n**The 584 instances breakdown:**\n- **1 call to `compress_rows`** (poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes) per Merkle tree built\n- **Multiple calls to `compress_layer`** (poseidon2_strided_compress_layer / poseidon2_adjacent_compress_layer) per tree\n\nIf you're seeing 584 total instances at APC 300, this represents:\n- Multiple trees being built (one per AIR commitment, possibly with different matrix widths)\n- Each tree has ~log(height) layers of compression\n\n### 4. Does result depend on per-AIR data?\n\n**YES, the results depend HEAVILY on per-AIR data:**\n\nThe kernel output is **not** constant — it depends on:\n\n1. **Column width** (`width` parameter, line 19): Controls Poseidon2 input size and number of mix() calls per row\n   - Different AIRs have different column widths\n   - Directly affects computation (line 41-50 in merkle_tree.cu)\n\n2. **Matrix height** (`height` parameter, line 20): Implicitly determines `query_stride = height / rows_per_query`\n   - Different traces have different heights\n   - Affects how many digests are output\n\n3. **`rows_per_query` and `log_rows_per_query`**: Control the Merkle tree depth (lines 53-71)\n   - Determined by `k_whir` parameter passed to tree constructor\n   - Could vary per AIR/commitment\n\n4. **Matrix data itself**: The actual row values being hashed\n   - Each AIR produces different witness data\n   - Completely determines the Poseidon2 output\n\n**Per-AIR dependency chain:**\n```\nAIR → Matrix (width, height, data)\n  → StackedLayout determines which columns from which AIRs\n  → stacked_commit() builds ONE Merkle tree per stack\n  → Within stack: rows_per_query = 1 << k_whir (constant)\n  → compress_rows() called ONCE per tree with AIR-specific width & data\n  → compress_layer() called log(query_stride) times per tree\n```\n\nSo the 584 instances do **NOT** compute the same value. Each instance computes a digest tree for a specific matrix (width, height, data), and the computation depends entirely on that matrix's per-AIR properties.\n\n### Key Files Summary:\n- **CUDA kernel source:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu` (16 kernels total)\n- **Rust bindings:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/merkle_tree.rs`\n- **Tree constructor:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs:113-172`\n- **Trace commit entry point:** `/home/