{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LUHEe9KfgVofZuqexTrUtF","type":"tool_result","content":"179\t) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>, Round0EvalError> {\n180\t    // Check if this trace has interactions\n181\t    if eq_3bs.is_empty() {\n182\t        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());\n183\t    }\n184\t    let large_domain = num_cosets * skip_domain;\n185\t\n186\t    // We create a new \"interactions DAG\" where the new .constraints are the interaction [count,\n187\t    // message_0, message_1, ..] expressions themselves, while the .interactions are empty\n188\t    // We track the indices with InteractionNode\n189\t\n190\t    // Copied from build_symbolic_constraints_dag to handle sorting of constraints\n191\t    // NOTE: For logup round0, the kernel uses weights indexed by rule_idx, not constraint_idx.\n192\t    // So we deduplicate constraint_idx and use dag_idx_to_rule_idx for weight mapping.\n193\t    let (rules, d_numer_weights, d_denom_weights, denom_sum_init) = {\n194\t        let mut dag_builder = SymbolicDagBuilder::new();\n195\t        let mut sorted_used_dag_idxs = Vec::new();\n196\t        for interaction in &symbolic.interactions {\n197\t            let count = dag_builder.add_expr(&interaction.count);\n198\t            sorted_used_dag_idxs.push(count);\n199\t            sorted_used_dag_idxs.extend(\n200\t                interaction\n201\t                    .message\n202\t                    .iter()\n203\t                    .map(|field_expr| dag_builder.add_expr(field_expr)),\n204\t            );\n205\t        }\n206\t        sorted_used_dag_idxs.sort();\n207\t        // Deduplicate for the dag since logup round0 kernel doesn't use used_nodes\n208\t        sorted_used_dag_idxs.dedup();\n209\t        let dag = SymbolicExpressionDag {\n210\t            nodes: dag_builder.nodes,\n211\t            constraint_idx: sorted_used_dag_idxs,\n212\t        };\n213\t        let rules = SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true);\n214\t        let mut numer_weights = vec![EF::ZERO; rules.rules.len()];\n215\t        let mut denom_weights = vec![EF::ZERO; rules.rules.len()];\n216\t        let mut denom_sum_init = EF::ZERO;\n217\t        for (interaction_idx, interaction) in symbolic.interactions.iter().enumerate() {\n218\t            // CAUTION: an expression node could be used in multiple interactions, and might even be\n219\t            // used as `count` in one, but message field in another. We only care about their\n220\t            // weighted sum with eq_3b, so we compute the weights ahead of time.\n221\t            let count_dag_idx =\n222\t                dag_builder.expr_to_idx[&(&interaction.count as *const SymbolicExpression<_>)];\n223\t            let count_rule_idx = rules.dag_idx_to_rule_idx[&count_dag_idx];\n224\t            numer_weights[count_rule_idx] += eq_3bs[interaction_idx];\n225\t            denom_sum_init += eq_3bs[interaction_idx]\n226\t                * beta_pows[interaction.message.len()]\n227\t                * F::from_u32(interaction.bus_index as u32 + 1);\n228\t\n229\t            for (message_idx, message) in interaction.message.iter().enumerate() {\n230\t                let message_dag_idx =\n231\t                    dag_builder.expr_to_idx[&(message as *const SymbolicExpression<_>)];\n232\t                let message_rule_idx = rules.dag_idx_to_rule_idx[&message_dag_idx];\n233\t                denom_weights[message_rule_idx] += eq_3bs[interaction_idx] * beta_pows[message_idx];\n234\t            }\n235\t        }\n236\t        let d_numer_weights = numer_weights.to_device()?;\n237\t        let d_denom_weights = denom_weights.to_device()?;\n238\t        (rules, d_numer_weights, d_denom_weights, denom_sum_init)\n239\t    };\n240\t\n241\t    let encoded_rules = rules.rules.iter().map(|c| c.encode()).collect_vec();\n242\t    let d_rules = encoded_rules.to_device()?;\n243\t\n244\t    let buffer_size: u32 = rules.buffer_size.try_into().unwrap();\n245\t    let intermed_capacity = unsafe {\n246\t        _logup_r0_intermediates_buffer_size(\n247\t            buffer_size,\n248\t            skip_domain,\n249\t            num_x,\n250\t            num_cosets,\n251\t            max_temp_bytes,\n252\t        )\n253\t    };\n254\t\n255\t    let use_prealloc_inter = prealloc_intermediates\n256\t        .as_ref()\n257\t        .map_or(false, |p| intermed_capacity > 0 && p.len() >= intermed_capacity);\n258\t    let mut fallback_intermediates;"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff03fa2a-06a9-4cae-9336-eb9eb1af38e2","uuid":"656462e4-84fd-4e79-b631-fffc27e22349","timestamp":"2026-04-14T05:23:30.254Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs","content":") -> Result<DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>, Round0EvalError> {\n    // Check if this trace has interactions\n    if eq_3bs.is_empty() {\n        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());\n    }\n    let large_domain = num_cosets * skip_domain;\n\n    // We create a new \"interactions DAG\" where the new .constraints are the interaction [count,\n    // message_0, message_1, ..] expressions themselves, while the .interactions are empty\n    // We track the indices with InteractionNode\n\n    // Copied from build_symbolic_constraints_dag to handle sorting of constraints\n    // NOTE: For logup round0, the kernel uses weights indexed by rule_idx, not constraint_idx.\n    // So we deduplicate constraint_idx and use dag_idx_to_rule_idx for weight mapping.\n    let (rules, d_numer_weights, d_denom_weights, denom_sum_init) = {\n        let mut dag_builder = SymbolicDagBuilder::new();\n        let mut sorted_used_dag_idxs = Vec::new();\n        for interaction in &symbolic.interactions {\n            let count = dag_builder.add_expr(&interaction.count);\n            sorted_used_dag_idxs.push(count);\n            sorted_used_dag_idxs.extend(\n                interaction\n                    .message\n                    .iter()\n                    .map(|field_expr| dag_builder.add_expr(field_expr)),\n            );\n        }\n        sorted_used_dag_idxs.sort();\n        // Deduplicate for the dag since logup round0 kernel doesn't use used_nodes\n        sorted_used_dag_idxs.dedup();\n        let dag = SymbolicExpressionDag {\n            nodes: dag_builder.nodes,\n            constraint_idx: sorted_used_dag_idxs,\n        };\n        let rules = SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true);\n        let mut numer_weights = vec![EF::ZERO; rules.rules.len()];\n        let mut denom_weights = vec![EF::ZERO; rules.rules.len()];\n        let mut denom_sum_init = EF::ZERO;\n        for (interaction_idx, interaction) in symbolic.interactions.iter().enumerate() {\n            // CAUTION: an expression node could be used in multiple interactions, and might even be\n            // used as `count` in one, but message field in another. We only care about their\n            // weighted sum with eq_3b, so we compute the weights ahead of time.\n            let count_dag_idx =\n                dag_builder.expr_to_idx[&(&interaction.count as *const SymbolicExpression<_>)];\n            let count_rule_idx = rules.dag_idx_to_rule_idx[&count_dag_idx];\n            numer_weights[count_rule_idx] += eq_3bs[interaction_idx];\n            denom_sum_init += eq_3bs[interaction_idx]\n                * beta_pows[interaction.message.len()]\n                * F::from_u32(interaction.bus_index as u32 + 1);\n\n            for (message_idx, message) in interaction.message.iter().enumerate() {\n                let message_dag_idx =\n                    dag_builder.expr_to_idx[&(message as *const SymbolicExpression<_>)];\n                let message_rule_idx = rules.dag_idx_to_rule_idx[&message_dag_idx];\n                denom_weights[message_rule_idx] += eq_3bs[interaction_idx] * beta_pows[message_idx];\n            }\n        }\n        let d_numer_weights = numer_weights.to_device()?;\n        let d_denom_weights = denom_weights.to_device()?;\n        (rules, d_numer_weights, d_denom_weights, denom_sum_init)\n    };\n\n    let encoded_rules = rules.rules.iter().map(|c| c.encode()).collect_vec();\n    let d_rules = encoded_rules.to_device()?;\n\n    let buffer_size: u32 = rules.buffer_size.try_into().unwrap();\n    let intermed_capacity = unsafe {\n        _logup_r0_intermediates_buffer_size(\n            buffer_size,\n            skip_domain,\n            num_x,\n            num_cosets,\n            max_temp_bytes,\n        )\n    };\n\n    let use_prealloc_inter = prealloc_intermediates\n        .as_ref()\n        .map_or(false, |p| intermed_capacity > 0 && p.len() >= intermed_capacity);\n    let mut fallback_intermediates;","numLines":80,"startLine":179,"totalLines":329}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MEYRByVoJhdfGFEFjx5Tro","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait — I see that the Round 0 interaction evaluation is STILL constructing the DAG per-AIR (lines 194-213)! The \"precompute-logup-round0-interaction-rules\" task was supposed to pre-compute this, but looking at this code, it's still doing it at runtime.\n\nLet me check: the previous task's report says \"The implementation followed the plan exactly\" and it refactored to use `pk.logup_round0`. But the code I'm reading still shows the DAG construction. Was the code reverted?\n\nLooking at the log.md entry:\n```\n## 2026-04-14-0300-precompute-logup-round0-interaction-rules\n\n\nResult: failure — Round 0 at APC 300: 243ms → 243ms (0ms change)\n```\n\nAnd looking at git log:\n```\n2e63a679 Revert \"Pre-compute logup Round 0 interaction rules at keygen time\"\n1756517f Pre-compute logup Round 0 interaction rules at keygen time\n```\n\nYes! The optimization was REVERTED (commit 2e63a679). So the code I'm reading is the post-revert code, which still constructs the DAG per-AIR in Round 0.\n\nOK, so the current state of the code includes per-AIR DAG construction and weight computation in Round 0 — this was found to only add about 5ms of overhead since the DAGs are small, so it was reverted.\n\nNow, back to my analysis. The key point is that `eq_3b_per_trace` is computed on CPU, then `d_eq_3b_per_trace` is uploaded to the device, and logup combinations are precomputed via GPU kernels. But Round 0 evaluation uses the host-side `eq_3bs` for weight computation, so both the precompute step and Round 0 need access to eq_3b data — one on the device side and one on the host side.\n\nSince both happen in the same function and Round 0 also needs the host-side eq_3b data, we can't overlap the precompute with Round 0. But wait — the host-side eq_3bs are available after the CPU computation, while the device-side version is available after the upload. The logup precompute uses the device-side data, and Round 0 uses the host-side data, so we could potentially reorder these steps to compute the host-side eq_3b first, then proceed with the device upload and precompute.\n\nThe key insight is that Round 0's per-AIR work doesn't depend on the device-side eq_3bs — it only uses the host-side versions for weight computation. This means we can start Round 0 on a separate stream while the precompute runs in parallel on the device, making the overlap feasible.\n\nBut there's a synchronization issue to resolve: the eq_3b upload to device buffers happens asynchronously, and the precompute kernel needs to read that data. If the upload and precompute are on different streams, we need explicit synchronization to ensure the upload completes before the kernel reads from those buffers.\n\nLet me step back and focus on what'll give the best performance improvement right now. Looking at the current bottlenecks at APC 300, LogUp GKR dominates at 39.3% of constraint time, followed by Round 0 at 15.2%. Round 0 has already seen significant optimization with multi-streaming and GPU extraction, so I should evaluate what other improvements are feasible.\n\nLogUp GKR's fractional sumcheck is inherently sequential, which limits parallelization gains. MLE Rounds have barely improved despite efforts, suggesting per-trace kernel overhead is the real constraint. Stacked Reduction got a solid 4.15x improvement from CPU NTT optimization, but Trace Commit's RS encoding path might be worth investigating more deeply since it's still at 19.1% of total time.\n\nLet me dig into the RS encoding bottleneck and see if there's a structural optimization I'm missing. or `stack_traces_into_expanded` — expands traces to codeword height\n2. `batch_ntt_small` — small inverse NTT\n3. `mle_interpolate_stages` — MLE interpolation\n4. `bit_rev` — bit-reversal permutation\n5. `batch_ntt` — forward NTT to get RS codewords\n\nThe NTT operations use `_CT_NTT` (ICICLE NTT) and `batch_ntt_kernel`. These are fundamental and hard to optimize.\n\nBut what about the MLE interpolation? What does `mle_interpolate_stages` do?\n\nActually, I realize I should look at this from a different angle. What if there's a way to SKIP some work at APC 300 that isn't needed?\n\nThe APC 300 configuration has 623 AIRs with 106,842 columns and 811M cells. Many of these AIRs are tiny auto-precompiles, so the per-AIR overhead of stacking, NTT, and Merkle tree leaf hashing might be disproportionate. But the stacking already batches columns together and the NTT processes all columns at once, so that's probably not the bottleneck.\n\nLet me settle on a concrete optimization: the 791 per-trace precompute calls happen sequentially on the default stream before Round 0 starts, taking about 37ms. I could batch the `precompute_logup_combinations` kernel launches by running them concurrently with Round 0 on a separate CUDA stream instead.\n\nActually, I realize the precompute happens inside `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` because the eq_3b values are fresh there. The MLE rounds later call `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` which uses these precomputed logup_combinations, so the precompute must complete before MLE rounds start. The real question is whether I can run it during Round 0 instead of before it.\n\nIf I move the precompute to after Round 0's multi-stream phase completes, it would still happen before MLE rounds, but that just shifts the 37ms delay rather than eliminating it. The key insight is to overlap the precompute on a separate CUDA stream while Round 0 is running — since Round 0 takes 165ms, the 37ms precompute would be hidden entirely. I'd need to spawn a thread that runs the precompute closure on a separate stream, let Round 0 proceed on the main stream, then join the precompute thread afterward and store the results.\n\nThe challenge is managing Rust's ownership rules for the borrowed fields the closure needs. Using `cudaStreamPerThread` with a scoped thread is the cleanest approach — it follows the same multi-stream pattern already used for Round 0 and GKR input evaluation. This should save roughly 37ms at APC 300 by hiding the precompute behind Round 0's execution, with smaller gains at lower APC values.\n\nLooking at the actual timing breakdown though, I'm realizing the 37ms savings might not be as impactful as I thought. The Round 0 span of 202ms already includes lambda power computation, lambda combination precomputation, and eq_3b computation, so I need to trace through more carefully where the actual bottleneck sits.\n\nThe real constraint is that step 8 (the multi-stream evaluation) takes about 150ms, which is longer than the 37ms logup combinations precompute, so overlapping those two would only save the full 37ms. There's also the question of whether the eq_3b H2D uploads are adding meaningful overhead—uploading nearly 800 small buffers totaling around 100KB could add latency depending on the per-upload cost.\n\nIf I can overlap steps 4 and 5 with step 8, I'd save roughly 45ms total, bringing Round 0 down from 202ms to about 157ms. That would put the full STARK (excluding trace generation) at around 1282ms, giving a scaling factor of about 1.67x instead of the current 1.5x. Still not hitting 2x though.\n\nI'm wondering if there's a bigger optimization I'm missing. The logup_combinations precompute happens early in Round 0, but then those values get reused in the MLE rounds. The eq_3b values do change per MLE round based on the challenges, but I need to check whether eq_3b_per_trace is actually being recomputed or if it's stable across rounds.\n\nLooking at the eq_3b computation, it's computing weights based on the xi challenge values that get sampled during the proof. Since those challenges are fixed after sampling, eq_3b gets computed once and then reused throughout all the MLE rounds. Same with logup_combinations — they're precomputed once and used consistently.\n\nI'm checking whether there's a resource conflict with the device-side eq_3b buffers. The Round 0 interaction evaluation uses host-side eq_3b values, not the device buffers, so the precompute phase is the only consumer of d_eq_3b_per_trace. That means there's no data dependency issue and the overlap approach should work cleanly.\n\nBut I need to think through the memory allocation more carefully. The precompute phase allocates temporary DeviceBuffers for the logup combinations through the CUDA memory pool, which uses mutex locking. The Round 0 threads also allocate buffers, but they rely on pre-allocated buffers from the optimization, so they avoid most pool contention.\n\nThe precompute allocations are small enough that they should complete quickly even with pool overhead. So this approach should work fine.\n\nI'm going to implement the optimization: run the logup combination precomputation on a separate thread with its own CUDA stream to overlap it with Round 0 multi-stream evaluation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the kernel launch overhead. The precompute launches 1582 tiny kernels sequentially, and while a separate stream would allow concurrent execution with Round 0, the sheer volume of kernel launches creates significant driver overhead that might not be worth the complexity.\n\nA better strategy is to batch the precompute into just 2 large kernel launches instead—one for numerators and one for denominators—and run those on a separate stream. This reduces launch overhead while still getting the benefit of overlapping with Round 0 computation. I'll use a descriptor array pattern to pass the necessary data to these batched kernels...\n\nWriting batched kernel code...\n\nThe expected savings would be around 37ms with full overlap, though bandwidth contention might reduce that to 25ms. Combined with batching the eq_3b uploads, I'm looking at roughly 40-45ms total savings.\n\nActually, the real insight here is simpler: the logup_combinations precompute is 37ms of sequential GPU work that happens before Round 0 even starts, and it's only needed for MLE rounds. Moving it to overlap with Round 0 eliminates that latency entirely without needing complex kernel changes.\n\nThe approach is straightforward: after uploading eq_3b, instead of running the precompute immediately, I'll start Round 0's multi-stream evaluation and spawn a thread to handle all the precompute calls concurrently. Once Round 0 finishes, I join the precompute thread. This saves roughly 30-35ms even without batching the precompute kernel itself, and I can optimize the kernel further in a follow-up.\n\nThe tricky part is handling Rust's ownership and thread safety when the precompute function allocates DeviceBuffers and returns them—I need to think through how to collect those results safely in the concurrent setup.\n\nSince the precompute kernels launch on the calling thread's CUDA stream, spawning a new thread means they'll use a different per-thread stream. The kernels read from d_eq_3b_per_trace, which was uploaded on the main thread's stream, so I need to synchronize between streams—either with a cudaStreamSynchronize after the uploads or by recording an event on the main stream and having the precompute thread wait on it. The synchronize approach is simpler.\n\nBut I'm realizing I can do better: the d_eq_3b upload and logup_combinations precompute aren't needed for Round 0, so I can move them to a background thread and overlap them with Round 0 itself. That means I'd do the lambda powers, lambda_combinations, eq_3b CPU compute, and eq_xis + sels on the main thread first, then spawn the background thread for the upload and precompute while Round 0 evaluation starts immediately.\n\nThe catch is that eq_3b CPU compute has to finish before Round 0 starts since it produces values needed for the weight computation inside the GPU evaluation. So I can only overlap the upload and precompute with Round 0, which saves about 45ms total. Let me think through whether there's any way to parallelize the CPU compute itself... steps 3+4 cost:\n- Step 3 (d_eq_3b upload): 791 small H2D copies. At APC 300, each trace has ~2-10 interactions, so eq_3b per trace is ~8-40 bytes × num_interactions. Let's say ~100 bytes on average × 791 = 79KB total. At GPU H2D bandwidth of ~10GB/s, this is ~8us. But the per-copy overhead dominates: 791 copies × ~5-10us each = ~4-8ms.\n- Step 4 (precompute): 1582 kernel launches, 37ms GPU time.\n\nTotal steps 3+4: ~42-45ms.\n\nIf overlapped with Round 0 (150ms), savings = 42-45ms.\n\nThis is a clean, impactful optimization. Now I need to verify the synchronization guarantees: the eq_3b uploads happen before the precompute kernel uses them on the same stream, so ordering is fine. The precompute output needs to be ready before MLE rounds start, which is guaranteed since we join the background thread before entering those rounds. The one thing I should double-check is that the device buffer allocations from the precompute thread are properly synchronized when the main thread accesses them during MLE — joining the thread should handle this implicitly.\n\nActually, I need to be more careful here. The allocations use `cudaMallocAsync` on the precompute thread's stream, which means they're only guaranteed to be visible on that stream. When the main thread accesses them on a different stream, I need explicit CUDA-level synchronization. Adding a `cudaStreamSynchronize` call at the end of the precompute thread before it returns will ensure all operations are complete and visible to other streams.\n\nI'm satisfied with this approach. Time to document the plan.\n\nLet me verify the data flow for Round 0 evaluation—specifically whether it truly runs independently of the logup precompute. Tracing through what Round 0 needs versus what the precompute computes, I'm checking for any hidden dependencies that might force serialization.\n\nThe key insight is that Round 0 uses HOST-side eq_3bs for CPU-based weight computation, while the precompute operates on DEVICE-side eq_3bs for GPU kernels—these are completely separate. The only shared resource is d_beta_pows, but since both operations only read from it, concurrent access is safe.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether this overlap strategy is even the right approach. Looking at the performance gap again—we're 254ms away from the 2x scaling target, and this optimization only closes about 45ms of that. I should examine whether there's a fundamentally different optimization path worth exploring instead.\n\nTrace Commit is the second-largest bottleneck at 254ms, and while it already scales well, the absolute time is still significant. Breaking down where that time goes: stack trace generation takes roughly 16ms, Reed-Solomon encoding with NTT is around 40ms, and Poseidon2 hashing for Merkle leaves dominates at nearly 191ms.\n\nThe Poseidon2 leaf hashing is the real problem here. The kernel hashes every column of each row using the Poseidon2 sponge, and at APC 300 with thousands of columns per stacking group, this becomes expensive. Each thread block processes one row group, with threads computing 16 hash absorptions per pass. The kernel is memory-bandwidth bound when reading the 3.2GB of trace data, but the actual hash computation itself—Poseidon2 at roughly 200 cycles per 16 absorptions—is where most of the time goes.\n\nWith 50.7M hash operations needed and the GPU achieving around 256M hashes per second, the math checks out to about 198ms, which matches the observed 191ms. So this is purely compute-bound, and the only realistic improvements would be reducing the data to hash, switching to a faster hash function, or further optimizing the Poseidon2 implementation—none of which are really feasible. Trace Commit is fundamentally bottlenecked by Poseidon2 compute.\n\nNow I'm looking at LogUp GKR, which takes 521ms and is the largest remaining component.\n\nThe fractional sumcheck itself takes about 233ms of GPU kernel time, but it's an inherent part of the GKR protocol with rounds scaling logarithmically, so there's limited room to optimize there. The real issue is the GKR input evaluation, which takes 288ms of wall time but only achieves 23% parallel efficiency across 8 threads processing 791 AIRs. That 77% overhead is coming from per-AIR setup costs like host-to-device transfers, weight computation, and buffer allocation—roughly 1-2ms per AIR, which adds up quickly across 50 AIRs per thread.\n\nIf I could cut that per-AIR overhead down to 0.5ms, I'd save about 75ms per thread, translating to roughly 75ms total wall time improvement. The main approaches are pre-computing weight arrays at keygen time, pre-uploading per-AIR data (which is already partially done), or batching small AIRs into single kernel launches. Batching would be most impactful but ran into issues when attempted for Round 0.\n\nI don't have solid profiling data on the actual per-AIR overhead in GKR input eval, so I'm going to stop second-guessing myself and commit to overlapping the precompute work with Round 0 instead—it's straightforward, well-understood, and should deliver 35-45ms of savings. Now I'll write up the task and implementation plan.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1763,"cache_read_input_tokens":144252,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1763},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff03fa2a-06a9-4cae-9336-eb9eb1af38e2","uuid":"afefbd30-2ae0-4a1c-b0b5-ff6b88f9c2fc"}
