flict.\n\nThe solution is to keep storing `eq_3b_per_trace` in `self` as originally planned, then compute the device-side `d_eq_3b_per_trace` and `logup_combinations` separately in a background thread that only needs the public key, device beta powers, and the already-computed eq_3b values—no need to borrow from `self` at all.\n\nRefactoring the approach... Before the scoped thread, collect all the data the background thread needs:\n\nActually, I'm realizing the lifetime issues make this unnecessarily complex. A simpler approach would be to skip the background thread entirely and instead integrate the logup precompute directly into the Round 0 multi-stream phase, which already has a thread pool running. I could add another task to that existing thread pool rather than spawning a separate background thread.\n\nThe idea is to modify the existing `thread::scope` block in Round 0 to spawn one additional thread that handles the logup precompute while the other threads process Round 0 work items. This thread would use its own CUDA stream and upload the necessary data, then join with the other threads when everything completes. The scope automatically handles synchronization, so there's no need to manually manage thread lifetimes or worry about data outliving the scope.\n\nFor the precompute thread, I need to pass read-only references to the public key, beta powers, the precomputed eq_3b values, and the per-trace context. The thread then produces the device buffers for d_eq_3b and the logup combinations, which get stored back in self after all threads finish. The borrow checker should be fine here since everything borrowed is immutable and the scope ensures all threads complete before any mutable access happens. compute thread and the Round 0 threads borrow `self.pk` and other shared references. Since these are all shared (&) references, this is fine.\n\nBut `self.eq_3b_per_trace` is a Vec stored in `self`, and the precompute thread reads it while the Round 0 threads also read it (via work items which borrow `&self.eq_3b_per_trace[trace_idx]`). Since both are shared reads, this is fine.\n\nThe only mutable access needed is to store the precompute results back into `self.d_eq_3b_per_trace` and `self.logup_combinations`. These are done AFTER both the precompute thread and Round 0 threads have completed (i.e., after `thread::scope` returns), so there's no concurrent mutation.\n\nThis should work cleanly with Rust's ownership model!\n\nNow I'm checking the actual borrows in the work items — they're capturing references to `self.pk.per_air`, `self.eq_3b_per_trace`, `self.eq_xis`, and `self.beta_pows`, while the precompute thread borrows the same fields. Since everything is shared reads with no overlapping mutable access, the borrow checker should be satisfied. Since the precompute takes only about 5ms while Round 0 takes 176ms, the concurrent execution won't provide meaningful speedup at APC 0. Now I'm ready to set up the task directory and document the implementation plan.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2706,"cache_read_input_tokens":159253,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2706},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff03fa2a-06a9-4cae-9336-eb9eb1af38e2","uuid":"7271e967-e202-4c09-89c2-71923bda258b"}
