uns on the background thread's `cudaStreamPerThread`, which is a distinct CUDA stream from each Round 0 worker thread's `cudaStreamPerThread`. There is no data sharing on the GPU side: the precompute reads from `self.d_beta_pows` and the uploaded `d_eq_3b` buffers, while Round 0 reads from trace data, eq_xi trees, selectors, and lambda powers -- all disjoint device memory regions. The background thread's `current_stream_sync()` before returning ensures all its GPU work is complete before the results are handed back to the main thread. The precompute produces identical results regardless of timing.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### 1. Batch the 791 small H2D uploads into a single transfer\n\nInstead of 791 individual `to_device()` calls (each allocating + copying ~100 EF elements), concatenate all `eq_3b_per_trace` vectors into a single host buffer, do one `to_device()` call, then slice the resulting device buffer into per-trace views. This eliminates ~790 `d_malloc` mutex acquisitions and ~790 `cudaMemcpyAsync` dispatch calls, converting launch-latency-dominated work into a single bulk transfer. The total data is ~600KB, easily handled in one copy. This would reduce the background thread's wall-clock time from ~42-45ms to perhaps ~5-10ms and also reduce mutex contention with Round 0 workers.\n\n### 2. Consider whether the precompute can be moved to before Round 0 setup entirely\n\nInstead of overlapping with Round 0, the precompute could be launched asynchronously right after `eq_3b_per_trace` is computed (line 944) and the `current_stream_sync` barrier (line 1192) would naturally wait for it before Phase 2 starts. The key insight is that the precompute kernels are launch-latency dominated; if batched (per improvement 1), the actual GPU work is negligible and the background thread complexity is unnecessary. The simpler change would be to batch the uploads and launch all precompute kernels on the default stream before the barrier. They would overlap with the